:title("Swift (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses a Swift subset and executes it directly, without generating code.

The subset: top level code that runs in source order (declare before use, no
hoisting), let/var declarations with an optional type annotation and an
optional initializer (types are parsed and ignored, let is not enforced, and an
uninitialized declaration starts as nil), functions with LABELLED parameters
(func greet(name: String, from town: String) -> String, called greet(name: x,
from: y)), default parameter values, variadic parameters, inout parameters,
@autoclosure, overloading by parameter type, nested functions and nested types,
single expression bodies with an implicit return, struct and class (and actor)
with stored properties, methods and an explicit init (self and implicit
unqualified property access inside methods, construction without new:
Counter(start: 10, step: 1); a struct without an init gets the memberwise one),
if/else (braces required), while, repeat/while, for-in over the ranges 1...5
(closed) and 1..<5 (half-open), over arrays and over dictionaries, an optional
where clause on a for, labelled loops with a labelled break/continue, guard,
switch over values, break/continue, closures { x in x * 2 } and
{ (a: Int, b: Int) -> Int in a + b } as values, closure capture lists, the
shorthand arguments dollar-zero and up, trailing closure call syntax (including
several labelled trailing closures), the array higher order methods map/filter/
forEach plus append/contains/count/first/last, arrays and dictionaries with
subscript read/write and .count, tuples (labelled or not, element access by
index or label, tuple patterns in let and in assignment, tuple return types),
string interpolation, the nil-coalescing operator a ?? b, optional chaining a?.b
and a?[i], force unwrap a!, nil, print(...) and return. Optional binding in
conditions: if let / guard let / while let (including the Swift 5.7 shorthand
if let x), compound comma conditions, if case / guard case patterns, a where
clause on a binding, and for case PAT in xs. defer { ... } runs when its block
is left - on the normal path, on a return and on a throw - in LIFO order. Int
arithmetic is 32 bit and / truncates; + concatenates when a side is a String;
== compares strings by value and objects by identity. Enums with bare cases, raw
values (E(rawValue:) and .rawValue) and associated values, plus enum methods and
computed properties; an enum case is matched and compared by its case NAME, which
is what makes the leading-dot form (.tails, .circle(r: 5)) work without a type
checker. Computed properties and willSet/didSet observers, static and class
members, subscripts (including multi-parameter and static ones), class
inheritance with overrides, super.m() / super.p / super.init(), is / as? / as!,
protocols whose extensions carry default implementations, extensions of a type
(including Int/String/Array), nested types reached as Outer.Inner, initializer
overloading and failable initializers, and struct VALUE semantics (a struct is
copied on binding, assignment and argument passing; self = X inside a mutating
method rewrites the value in place). switch matches PATTERNS: values, ranges
(1...9, ..<0, 10...), tuples, enum cases with their associated values, bindings
and a where guard, with fallthrough. Error handling with do { STMTS } catch
PATTERN [where COND] { STMTS } and throw EXPR (no finally; the first clause whose
pattern matches wins, an unmatched error keeps unwinding, an unbound catch binds
the implicit `error`); try and try! are parsed and ignored (the throw happens
inside the call) and try? yields nil instead of throwing. import resolves a project module to a file
that is parsed and walked into the same scope. Conditional compilation
(#if / #elseif / #else / #endif, at top level, in a block, in a type body, in a
closure body and around the cases of a switch) is STRUCTURAL: the condition is
read as raw text to the end of its line and never evaluated, every branch is
parsed like ordinary code, and the first branch whose condition is not literally
false (or 0) is the one that runs - which for a real condition such as
os(macOS), canImport(Foundation) or swift(>=5.7) is always the first branch.
A declaration made under #if stays visible after the #endif. #available and
#unavailable answer true and false; #sourceLocation / #error / #warning /
#assert are accepted and ignored, and a leading hashbang line is skipped.

Every numeric literal form (hexadecimal, octal, binary, digit group separators,
decimal and hexadecimal floats with exponents) and every string literal form
(unicode escapes, multi-line strings with the closing delimiter's indentation
stripped, raw strings with one or two pound levels and their pound-interpolation).
A string is a sequence of extended grapheme clusters: .count counts CLUSTERS and
== compares by CANONICAL EQUIVALENCE, so a precomposed letter equals its
decomposed spelling. lazy properties (built at the first read), property wrappers
(@Wrapper var x, with x reading wrappedValue and $x projectedValue), operator and
precedencegroup declarations with user-defined infix and prefix operators folded
by their declared precedence and associativity, operator overloading through
`static func + / == / prefix -` on a type, the bitwise and overflow operators,
and ranges as values (1...5 ~= 3, for-in over a range value).

The value model: a number carries its own type. An Int is a plain value at its
declared width (Int is 64 bit; Int8 ... UInt64 are exactly what they say and wrap
at their width - real Swift traps instead), and a Double is a BOX, so 7.0 / 2.0 is
3.5 where 7 / 2 is 3 and a whole Double still prints 3.0 rather than 3. A mixed
Int/Double operation evaluates in floating point, which is real Swift's answer
when a literal is involved and a compile error otherwise - this front end has no
type checker to tell the two apart. Float is a real IEEE-754 binary32. A user-defined infix operator sits at one level of the expression
grammar, binding tighter than * and /, and the precedence groups order the custom
operators among THEMSELVES. A compound assignment is lowered to a = a <op> b, so
an overloaded + is what runs for +=, and a declared `static func +=` is accepted
but unused. Canonical equivalence covers the Latin-1 precomposed letters.

Not in the subset (accepted structurally and reported as not implemented under
-warn-unsupported, or simply not parsed): generics (parsed and ignored),
async/await (async and actor declarations are accepted and their bodies never
awaited), macros, result builders, raw multi-line strings and pound levels beyond
two, suppressed conformances (~Copyable is parsed and contributes nothing), and
parameter packs.

Top level self checking programs count failures and end with exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
// Swift's whitespace, spelled HERE rather than taken from lib/cstyle-comments.abnf,
// because Swift accepts more of it than C does. The real lexer treats a form feed
// (0x0C) and a vertical tab (0x0B) as plain whitespace, and it accepts a
// NON-BREAKING SPACE (U+00A0) and its Unicode relatives with a warning and a fix-it
// rather than an error - `let x\u{a0}= 3` compiles. A NUL byte is the same story: it
// warns "nul character embedded in middle of file" and lexes on. The lib file backs
// ~15 grammars where most of that would be wrong, so the divergence lives here.
// Everything else is the lib file verbatim.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n\x0c\x0b\x00" | SwUniSpace | Comment } ;
SwUniSpace  = "\u00a0" | "\u1680" | "\u2000"..."\u200a" | "\u202f" | "\u205f" | "\u3000" | "\ufeff" ;
Comment     = LineComment | BlockComment ;
LineComment = "//" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// Swift's identifiers, spelled HERE rather than taken from lib/ident.abnf, because
// Swift accepts far more than ASCII in a name: `case 😀` and `let π = 3` are both
// ordinary declarations (Swift's rule is a Unicode category set close to XID_Start /
// XID_Continue). The approximation below is "any scalar at U+00A1 or above except
// the ones this grammar lexes as WHITESPACE" - the space characters have to be cut
// out, or a non-breaking space after a name would be absorbed into it. KwEnd and
// IdBacktick are the lib file verbatim.
// A backtick-quoted name (`protocol`, `self`) is the same name as the bare word;
// every site that records a name's text strips the quotes with swBt.
Id          = IdBacktick | IdPlain ;
IdPlain     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" | IdUni ) :whitespace()
              { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" | IdUni } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// The scalars Swift reserves for OPERATORS (OpUni) are cut out as well, so the
// square root sign before a name is a prefix operator applied to it.
IdUni       = "\u00a8" | "\u00aa" | "\u00ad" | "\u00af" | "\u00b2"..."\u00b5" | "\u00b7"..."\u00ba"
            | "\u00bc"..."\u00be" | "\u00c0"..."\u00d6" | "\u00d8"..."\u00f6" | "\u00f8"..."\u167f"
            | "\u1681"..."\u1fff" | "\u200b"..."\u2015" | "\u2018"..."\u201f" | "\u202a"..."\u202e"
            | "\u203f"..."\u2040" | "\u2054" | "\u2060"..."\u218f" | "\u2400"..."\u24ff"
            | "\u2776"..."\u2793" | "\u2c00"..."\u2dff" | "\u2e80"..."\u2fff" | "\u3004"..."\u3007"
            | "\u3021"..."\u302f" | "\u3031"..."\ufefe" | "\uff00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
KwEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        return ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95 || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122))
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01keyword-boundary", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
IdBacktick  = "`" :whitespace() IdBtChar { IdBtChar } "`" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IdBtChar    = "\x20"..."\x5f" | "\x61"..."\U0010ffff" ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Every top level item pushes a thunk (or nothing), a declaration a {decl}
// record. TopExec runs a declaration as soon as it is walked and queues the
// statements, which the Program tag runs in source order once the whole file is
// walked - so every declaration is registered before the first statement runs.
// The indirection is what lets a #if branch collect its items WITHOUT running
// them - see PoundIfTop, which splices the live branch's thunks into the
// enclosing item list.
//
// swRunTop, not the loop written out here, because an error NOBODY CAUGHT has to
// be reported as a diagnostic rather than allowed to escape into the engine - see
// its comment in the start script below.
// The Program tag then runs the TOP-LEVEL defers, once: a `defer` marker is
// parked in a script-side list as its statement runs and drained here. swiftc
// runs them at the end of the top level, LIFO, and only on the normal path - see
// swRunTop.
Program     <~~ swRunQueued(); swRunTopDefers() ~~>
            = [ Hashbang ] { TopExec } ;
TopExec     <~~ swRunTop(takeAll()) ~~>
            = TopItem ;
TopItem     = PoundIfTop StmtSep | OpDeclTop StmtSep | FunTop StmtSep | TypeTop StmtSep
            | UnsupTop StmtSep | TopStmt ;
// An operator / precedencegroup declaration is matched BEFORE the modifier-led
// items: `prefix` is in the modifier list (it precedes `func`), so a greedy
// Modifiers would eat the `prefix` of `prefix operator ~~~` and leave nothing.
// A top-level DECLARATION is a {decl} record, run as soon as it is walked, while
// the statements around it wait for the end of the file (swRunTop / swRunQueued):
// main.swift's declarations are visible to the whole file, so `print(f(2))` above
// `func f` calls it.
OpDeclTop   <~~ swDeclWrap(takeAll()) ~~> = PrecGroupDecl | OperatorDecl ;
FunTop      <~~ push({decl: makeRegFun(pop())}) ~~> = [ Modifiers ] FunDecl ;
TypeTop     <~~ swDeclWrap(takeAll()) ~~> = [ Modifiers ] ( StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl ) ;
UnsupTop    <~~ swDeclWrap(takeAll()) ~~> = [ Modifiers ] UnsupDecl ;
TopStmt     = Statement ;

// ----- #if / #elseif / #else / #endif conditional compilation -----
// Structural only: the condition is consumed as raw text to the end of its line
// and NOT evaluated. Every branch is parsed like ordinary code (so an unparsed
// construct in an inactive branch is still reported), and exactly one branch is
// kept: the first whose condition is not the literal `false` / `0`, which for a
// real condition like os(macOS) or canImport(Foo) is always the FIRST branch.
// The kept branch's items are spliced into the enclosing list, so a declaration
// made under #if is visible after the #endif, as in Swift.
PoundIfTop  <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyTop { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyTop }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyTop ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyTop <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { TopItem } ;
// A statement-level #if lives in the item list of a block (and of a switch body),
// not in Statement itself, because it can contribute more than one statement.
PoundIfStmt <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyStmt { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyStmt }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyStmt ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyStmt <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { PoundIfStmt | Statement } ;
PoundIfMember <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyMember { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyMember }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyMember ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyMember <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { Member } ;
// A #if around case labels of a switch: its branches hold labels AND statements.
PoundIfCase <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyCase { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyCase }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyCase ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyCase <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { PoundIfCase | CaseLabel | Statement } ;
// A #if inside a closure body, whose items are the {stmt} / {expr} lambda records.
PoundIfLam  <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyLam { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyLam }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyLam ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyLam <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { PoundIfLam | LamStmt } ;
// #elseif is spelled before #else on purpose: as a PEG, "#else" would match the
// prefix of "#elseif" and leave a stray `if` to be read as an if statement.
PoundIf     = "#if" ;
PoundElseIf = "#elseif" ;
PoundElse   <~~ push({pcond: "1"}) ~~> = "#else" ;
PoundEndif  = "#endif" ;
// The condition: every byte up to (not including) the end of the line, with
// whitespace skipping switched off so the run cannot cross the line break.
PoundCond   <~~ push({pcond: up.in}) ~~>
            = :whitespace() { PoundCondChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PoundCondChar = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b" | "\x0c" | "\x0e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// An INACTIVE clause of a VERSION check is not parsed at all: swiftc skips the body
// of a clause that is off when its condition (or, for #else, the one before it)
// mentions compiler(...), swift(...) or the legacy _compiler_version("..."), so code
// for a newer compiler may sit there
// (`#if compiler(>=99) ... #else`). PoundSkipScan decides that from the source
// alone - it walks back to the chain's #if, re-evaluates every clause up to this
// one exactly as poundPick does, and then returns the raw body up to the next
// directive at the same depth as ONE token; any other clause makes it fail.
PoundSkip   = :whitespace() PoundSkipScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PoundSkipScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var ev = function(s) {
            var vals = []
            var ops = []
            var i = 0
            var n = s.length
            var bad = false
            var guardN = 0
            while (i < n && !bad && guardN < 10000) {
                guardN++
                var ch = s.charCodeAt(i)
                if (ch == 32 || ch == 9 || ch == 13 || ch == 10) { i++; continue }
                if (ch == 47) { i = n; continue }
                if (ch == 33 || ch == 40) { ops.push(ch == 33 ? "!" : "("); i++; continue }
                var isAnd = ch == 38 && i + 1 < n && s.charCodeAt(i + 1) == 38
                var isOr = ch == 124 && i + 1 < n && s.charCodeAt(i + 1) == 124
                if (ch == 41 || isAnd || isOr) {
                    var stopAt = ch == 41 ? 0 : (isAnd ? 2 : 1)
                    while (ops.length > 0 && ops[ops.length - 1] != "(" && vals.length >= 2
                           && (ops[ops.length - 1] == "&" ? 2 : 1) >= stopAt) {
                        var o = ops.pop()
                        var rb = vals.pop()
                        var ra = vals.pop()
                        if (o == "&") { vals.push(ra == 0 || rb == 0 ? 0 : (ra == 1 && rb == 1 ? 1 : 2)) }
                        else { vals.push(ra == 1 || rb == 1 ? 1 : (ra == 0 && rb == 0 ? 0 : 2)) }
                    }
                    if (ch == 41) {
                        if (ops.length == 0) { bad = true; continue }
                        ops.pop()
                        while (ops.length > 0 && ops[ops.length - 1] == "!" && vals.length > 0) {
                            ops.pop()
                            var nv = vals.pop()
                            vals.push(nv == 2 ? 2 : 1 - nv)
                        }
                        i++
                    } else {
                        ops.push(isAnd ? "&" : "|")
                        i += 2
                    }
                    continue
                }
                var st = i
                while (i < n) {
                    var cc = s.charCodeAt(i)
                    if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122)
                        || cc == 95 || cc == 36 || cc == 46 || cc >= 128) { i++ } else { break }
                }
                if (i == st) { bad = true; continue }
                var name = s.substring(st, i)
                var arg = ""
                var j = i
                while (j < n && (s.charCodeAt(j) == 32 || s.charCodeAt(j) == 9)) { j++ }
                if (j < n && s.charCodeAt(j) == 40) {
                    var depth = 0
                    var k = j
                    while (k < n) {
                        var ck = s.charCodeAt(k)
                        if (ck == 40) { depth++ }
                        if (ck == 41) { depth--; if (depth == 0) { break } }
                        k++
                    }
                    arg = s.substring(j + 1, k)
                    i = k + 1
                }
                var v = 2
                if (name == "true" || name == "1") { v = 1 }
                if (name == "false" || name == "0") { v = 0 }
                if (name == "_compiler_version") { v = 1 }
                if ((name == "compiler" || name == "swift" || name == "_compiler_version") && arg != "") {
                    var a = ""
                    for (var q = 0; q < arg.length; q++) { if (arg.charCodeAt(q) != 32) { a += arg.charAt(q) } }
                    var ge = a.substring(0, 2) == ">="
                    var lt = a.substring(0, 1) == "<"
                    if (ge || lt) {
                        var want = (ge ? a.substring(2) : a.substring(1)) + "."
                        var have = (name == "swift" ? "5.10" : "6.1.2") + "."
                        var cmp = 0
                        var wn = 0
                        var hn = 0
                        var wi = 0
                        var hi = 0
                        while (cmp == 0 && (wi < want.length || hi < have.length)) {
                            wn = 0
                            hn = 0
                            while (wi < want.length && want.charCodeAt(wi) != 46) { wn = wn * 10 + (want.charCodeAt(wi) - 48); wi++ }
                            while (hi < have.length && have.charCodeAt(hi) != 46) { hn = hn * 10 + (have.charCodeAt(hi) - 48); hi++ }
                            wi++
                            hi++
                            if (hn > wn) { cmp = 1 }
                            if (hn < wn) { cmp = 0 - 1 }
                        }
                        v = ge ? (cmp >= 0 ? 1 : 0) : (cmp < 0 ? 1 : 0)
                    }
                }
                vals.push(v)
                while (ops.length > 0 && ops[ops.length - 1] == "!" && vals.length > 0) {
                    ops.pop()
                    var mv = vals.pop()
                    vals.push(mv == 2 ? 2 : 1 - mv)
                }
            }
            while (!bad && ops.length > 0 && vals.length >= 2) {
                var o2 = ops.pop()
                if (o2 == "(" || o2 == "!") { bad = true; continue }
                var b2 = vals.pop()
                var a2 = vals.pop()
                if (o2 == "&") { vals.push(a2 == 0 || b2 == 0 ? 0 : (a2 == 1 && b2 == 1 ? 1 : 2)) }
                else { vals.push(a2 == 1 || b2 == 1 ? 1 : (a2 == 0 && b2 == 0 ? 0 : 2)) }
            }
            if (bad || vals.length != 1 || ops.length != 0) { return 2 }
            return vals[0]
        }
        // The directive word of the line starting at relative offset ls (`#elseif`
        // gives "elseif"), "" when the line holds none; rest is the text after it.
        var word = function(ls) {
            var p = ls
            while (c.peek(p) === 32 || c.peek(p) === 9) { p++ }
            if (c.peek(p) !== 35) { return "" }
            p++
            var w = ""
            while (c.peek(p) >= 97 && c.peek(p) <= 122) { w += String.fromCharCode(c.peek(p)); p++ }
            return w
        }
        var rest = function(ls) {
            var p = ls
            while (c.peek(p) !== 35) { p++ }
            p++
            while (c.peek(p) >= 97 && c.peek(p) <= 122) { p++ }
            var t = ""
            while (c.peek(p) >= 0 && c.peek(p) !== 10) { t += String.fromCharCode(c.peek(p)); p++ }
            return t
        }
        var lineStart = function(p0) {
            var p = p0
            var cap = 0
            while (c.peek(p - 1) >= 0 && c.peek(p - 1) !== 10 && cap < 1000000) { p--; cap++ }
            return p
        }
        var nextLine = function(p0) {
            var p = p0
            var cap = 0
            while (c.peek(p) >= 0 && c.peek(p) !== 10 && cap < 1000000) { p++; cap++ }
            return c.peek(p) < 0 ? p : p + 1
        }
        var cur = lineStart(0)
        var cw = word(cur)
        if (cw !== "if" && cw !== "elseif" && cw !== "else") { return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-clause", 0) }
        var start = cur
        var depth = 0
        var guardN = 0
        while (cw !== "if" && guardN < 1000000) {
            guardN++
            if (c.peek(start - 1) < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01no-chain", 0) }
            start = lineStart(start - 1)
            var bw = word(start)
            if (bw === "endif") { depth++ }
            if (bw === "if") { if (depth === 0) { break } ; depth-- }
        }
        var found = false
        var isVer = false
        var skip = false
        var p = start
        depth = 0
        guardN = 0
        while (guardN < 1000000) {
            guardN++
            var w = word(p)
            if ((w === "if" && p === start) || (depth === 0 && (w === "elseif" || w === "else"))) {
                var active = false
                if (w === "else") { active = !found }
                else {
                    var cond = rest(p)
                    isVer = cond.indexOf("compiler(") >= 0 || cond.indexOf("swift(") >= 0
                            || cond.indexOf("_compiler_version(") >= 0
                    active = !found && ev(cond) !== 0
                }
                if (active) { found = true }
                if (p === cur) { skip = !active && isVer; break }
            }
            if (w === "if" && p !== start) { depth++ }
            if (w === "endif") { depth-- }
            p = nextLine(p)
        }
        if (!skip) { return abnf.newToken("\x01clause-is-parsed", 0) }
        var end = nextLine(0)
        depth = 0
        guardN = 0
        while (c.peek(end) >= 0 && guardN < 1000000) {
            guardN++
            var ew = word(end)
            if (ew === "if") { depth++ }
            if (depth === 0 && (ew === "elseif" || ew === "else" || ew === "endif")) { break }
            if (ew === "endif") { depth-- }
            end = nextLine(end)
        }
        var s = ""
        var q = 0
        while (q < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(q)
            if (b0 < 128) { s = s + String.fromCharCode(b0); q = q + 1 }
            else if (b0 < 224) {
                s = s + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(q + 1) - 128))
                q = q + 2
            }
            else if (b0 < 240) {
                s = s + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(q + 1) - 128) * 64)
                                            + (c.peek(q + 2) - 128))
                q = q + 3
            }
            else {
                var cp = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(q + 1) - 128) * 4096)
                         + ((c.peek(q + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(q + 3) - 128) - 65536
                s = s + String.fromCharCode(55296 + Math.floor(cp / 1024))
                s = s + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (cp - Math.floor(cp / 1024) * 1024))
                q = q + 4
            }
        }
        return abnf.newToken(s, 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// #sourceLocation(...) / #error(...) / #warning(...): accepted and ignored.
PoundDirective <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = PoundDirName SkipParen ;
PoundDirName = "#sourceLocation" | "#error" | "#warning" | "#assert" ;
// The same directives written inside a type body; the class builder ignores the
// empty member record they push, exactly as it ignores deinit's.
MemberDirective <~~ push({}) ~~> = PoundDirName SkipParen ;
// A #! line is only a hashbang on the FIRST line of the file, which is where
// Program matches it.
Hashbang    = "#!" :whitespace() { PoundCondChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Declaration modifiers and attributes (@objc, @available(...), @escaping) are
// parsed and ignored.
Modifiers   = { Modifier | Attribute } ;
Attribute   = "@" Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } [ SkipParen ] ;
Modifier    = AccessMod
            | ( "prefix" | "postfix" | "infix"
              | "final" | "static" | "mutating" | "nonmutating"
              | "override" | "lazy" | "weak" | "unowned" | "convenience"
              | "required" | "dynamic" | "indirect"
              | "nonisolated" | "distributed" | "borrowing" | "consuming" | "__consuming" ) KwEnd ;
// An access level, optionally for the setter alone: `private(set) var x = 0`.
AccessMod   = ( "public" | "private" | "fileprivate" | "internal" | "open" | "package" ) KwEnd
              [ "(" "set" ")" ] ;
// `class` is a type-member modifier (class var / class func) as well as a
// declaration keyword, so it is NOT in Modifier - a greedy Modifiers would eat the
// `class` of `@objc class Cache { ... }` and leave nothing for ClassDecl. Member
// tries the nested-type alternative first and only then this list.
// A member's modifiers push a marker for the ones the type builder needs (static /
// class puts the member on the type descriptor instead of on instances); the rest
// are parsed and dropped. A marker arrives ahead of the member's own record, in
// source order, so splitMembers attaches it to the member that follows.
MemberModifiers = { MemStatic | MemLazy | MemMut | MemAttr | Modifier | ClassMod } ;
MemStatic   <~~ push({mstatic: 1}) ~~> = ( "static" | "class" ) KwEnd ;
MemLazy     <~~ push({mlazy: 1}) ~~> = "lazy" KwEnd ;
MemMut      <~~ push({mmut: 1}) ~~> = "mutating" KwEnd ;
// A member attribute keeps its NAME: @Wrapper on a stored property is a property
// wrapper, and everything else (@objc, @escaping, ...) is parsed and dropped. An
// argument list that reads as call arguments is kept too: `@Clamped(0, 10) var x
// = 5` constructs the wrapper as Clamped(wrappedValue: 5, 0, 10).
MemAttr     = "@" Id <~~ push({mattr: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> { "." Id } [ MemAttrArgs | SkipParen ] ;
MemAttrArgs <~~ push({mattrArgs: takeAll()}) ~~> = "(" [ CallArg { "," CallArg } ] ")" ;
ClassMod    = "class" KwEnd ;

// func name(label param: Type, ...) -> Ret { ... }
FunDecl     <~~ var fd = makeFun(takeAll(), up.start, up.pos); swInitClaim(up.start, up.pos, false); swNoteTop(fd.name); push(fd) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName [ FunGenParams ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ "->" FunRetType <~~ push({rtxt: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;
// AnnType, except that a type no adoption acts on is captured as TEXT too: the
// member-chain typer (swChainAnns) reads a function's declared return type.
FunRetType  = FnAnnType | LabelledTupleType | IntAnnType | OptNumAnnType | DeepAnnType | OtherAnnType ;

// A function/method/init body. Swift returns the value of a body that is a SINGLE
// expression without writing `return`, so the one-expression form is tried first;
// anything else is an ordinary block. ExprBody is ordered first on purpose - a body
// that starts with a declaration or a `return` fails it immediately.
// RetBody is Block with a leading `return` REQUIRED, ordered ahead of ExprBody
// because `return (1, 2)` would otherwise parse as one expression - the identifier
// `return` applied to a tuple.
FnBody      = RetBody | ExprBody | Block ;
ExprBody    <~~ swAnnClose(); var eb = takeAll(); push(makeExprBody(swDotPend(eb[0], eb[1], up.pos))) ~~>
            = "{" <~~ swAnnOpen() ~~> Expression <~~ push({itext: up.in, iat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> "}" ;
// `throws(E)` is a TYPED throw (SE-0413); the thrown value is the same either way,
// so the type is parsed and dropped.
FnEffects   = { "throws" KwEnd [ ThrownType ] | ( "rethrows" | "async" ) KwEnd } ;
ThrownType  = NoSpaceParen "(" !")" Type ")" ;
// A generic constraint clause on a function/type: where T: P, U == V (ignored).
GenWhere    = KwWhere GenConstraint { "," GenConstraint } ;
GenConstraint = Type ( ":" TypeList | "==" Type | "=" Type ) ;

ParamList   <~~ var ps = takeAll(); swAnnParams(ps); push({params: ps}) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// A parameter is [externalLabel] internalName : Type; the last identifier is
// the bound name (Swift's _ label is just an identifier here).
// With two identifiers the first is the ARGUMENT LABEL and the second the bound
// name; with one, the label and the name are the same. The label `_` means "no
// label". The declared type is kept as text, for picking between overloads.
Param       <~~ push(makeParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { ParamLead } Id <~~ push({pid: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> [ Id <~~ push({pid: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> ] ":" [ ParamInout ] { ParamAttr } ParamType [ ParamVariadic ] [ "=" ParamDflt ] ;
ParamInout  <~~ push({pinout: 1}) ~~> = "inout" KwEnd ;
// A DECLARATION attribute in front of the parameter name - `func f(@_noImplicitCopy
// _ x: K)`. It contributes nothing here, so it pushes nothing; its argument list (if
// it has one) is adjacent, which is what keeps `@escaping (Int) -> Int` in the
// parameter TYPE position from losing its function type to SkipParen.
ParamLead   = "@" Id { "." Id } [ SkipAngle ] [ NoSpaceParen SkipParen ] ;
ParamType   <~~ push({ptype: up.in}) ~~> = Type ;
ParamVariadic <~~ push({pvar: 1}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamDflt   <~~ push({pdflt: pop(), dat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> = Expression ;
// Parameter/type attributes (@escaping, @autoclosure) take no argument list, so
// the ( ... ) that follows belongs to a function type. The few type attributes
// that DO take one (`@isolated(any)`, `@convention(c)`) are named, which is how
// swiftc tells `@convention(c) () -> Int` from `@escaping(Int) -> Int` too.
ParamAttr   = "@" ( "isolated" NoSpaceParen "(" "any" KwEnd ")"
                  | ArgAttrName NoSpaceParen SkipParen
                  | Id <~~ push({pattr: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> { "." Id } ) ;
ArgAttrName = ( "convention" | "differentiable" | "_opaqueReturnTypeOf" ) KwEnd ;

// class / struct with stored properties, methods and an explicit init.
StructDecl  <~~ push(makeStructStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStruct Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeGenParams ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwClass Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeGenParams ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A generic function's parameter names, open while its declaration is walked
// (swGenOpen in lib/swift-dotwant.js; makeFun closes them).
FunGenParams <~~ swGenOpen(swGenParamNames(stripWs(up.in)), up.start) ~~> = SkipAngle ;
// A generic type's parameter NAMES (constraints dropped): swGenMark in
// lib/swift-dotwant.js binds them per instance.
TypeGenParams <~~ push({tgens: swGenParamNames(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = SkipAngle ;
// The inheritance clause: for a class its first entry may be the SUPERCLASS, and
// every entry may be a protocol whose default implementations are mixed in. Each
// name is kept as text and resolved when the type is created.
InheritList = InheritName { "," InheritName } ;
// A leading ~ is a SUPPRESSED conformance (~Copyable, ~Escapable): it is recorded
// under its written name, which resolves to nothing, so it contributes nothing.
InheritName <~~ push({inherit: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = [ "~" ] Type ;
// An actor is a class with an isolation domain. There is no executor in this
// runtime (async functions are defined, never awaited), so it lowers like a class.
ActorDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwActor Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeGenParams ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
KwActor     = "actor" KwEnd ;
TypeList    = Type { "," Type } ;

// A nested type is matched BEFORE the modifier-led members, so the `class` of
// `class Inner {}` is read as the declaration keyword and the `class` of
// `class var kind` as a modifier (ClassDecl fails on the `var` that follows).
Member      = PoundIfMember | MemberDirective | MemberSemi | MemberType | [ MemberModifiers ] ( CaseDecl | InitDecl | DeinitDecl | SubscriptDecl | MethodDecl | ComputedProp | PropDecl ) ;
// A stray `;` between members - `enum E { enum F { case x }; case a }`. It declares
// nothing, so it pushes the same empty member record a #directive does.
MemberSemi  <~~ push({}) ~~> = ";" ;
// The nested declaration's own statement is dropped: a nested type is accepted
// structurally, not yet reachable as Outer.Inner.
// nidx carries the WALK-time INDEX of the type just declared (an index, never the
// record - a value pushed onto the tag stack is deep-copied), which is how the
// enclosing declaration marks it NESTED. A TypeAliasDecl clears lastDeclIdx, so it
// contributes none. See declType for what nesting changes.
MemberType  <~~ push({nested: pop(), nidx: lastDeclIdx}) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] ( StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl | TypeAliasDecl ) ;
InitDecl    <~~ swDotClaim(up.start, up.pos, ""); swInitClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); push({method: makeInit(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwInit [ "?" | "!" ] [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;
// deinit is accepted and ignored - this runtime is garbage collected, so there is
// no deterministic point at which to run it.
DeinitDecl  <~~ push({}) ~~> = KwDeinit SkipBlock ;
// subscript(i: Int) -> Int { get { ... } set { ... } } becomes the pair of ordinary
// methods __sub_get / __sub_set, which is what [i] reads and writes dispatch to. A
// second subscript of a different arity is an overload of the same pair.
SubscriptDecl <~~ swDotClaim(up.start, up.pos, ""); var ms = makeSubscript(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < ms.length; i++) { push(ms[i]) } ~~>
            = KwSubscript [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] PropAccessors ;
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll(), up.start, up.pos)}) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName [ FunGenParams ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ "->" AnnType <~~ push({rtxt: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;
// var w = 2, h = 3 declares two stored properties; each binding becomes its own
// {prop} record, so the type builder sees them in declaration order. In `var w,
// h: Double` both are Double (swBindsShared in lib/swift-dotwant.js).
PropDecl    <~~ var pvar = up.in.substring(0, 3) == "var"; var pbs = swBindsShared(takeAll(), function(bi) { return makeProp(bi, pvar) }); for (var pb = 0; pb < pbs.length; pb++) { push(pbs[pb]) } ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd PropBind { "," PropBind } [ ";" ] ;
PropBind    <~~ push({dbind: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ ":" DeclAnnType ]
              [ "=" Expression <~~ push({itext: up.in, iat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> ] ;
// A computed property (`var scaled: Int { get { ... } set { ... } }`, or the
// getter-only shorthand `var family: String { "beast" }`) and a stored property
// with willSet/didSet observers - the two share one syntax, told apart by which
// accessors the braces hold. An observed property keeps its storage under a
// hidden name and gets a generated getter/setter, so every property read and
// write goes through the same accessor lookup.
ComputedProp <~~ push(makeComputed(takeAll(), up.start, up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ ":" DeclAnnType ] [ "=" PropDefault ] PropAccessors ;
PropDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
// The accessor list is tried first: `get` and `set` are ordinary identifiers, so a
// getter-only body would otherwise read `get { ... }` as a trailing-closure call.
// Each alternative carries its own braces, so a body that is NOT an accessor list
// makes the whole alternative fail and the getter shorthand is reached (the same
// shape FnBody uses - an ordered choice never retries a committed alternative).
// A brace run this grammar cannot read as accessors (a coroutine accessor's
// `_modify { yield &x }`, an unsupported effect specifier) is still ACCEPTED
// structurally, as the last alternative: the declaration keeps its stored behavior
// instead of failing the whole file.
PropAccessors = AccessorBlock | ImplicitGetter | OpaqueAccessors ;
OpaqueAccessors <~~ push({}) ~~> = SkipBlock ;
AccessorBlock = "{" Accessor { Accessor } "}" ;
Accessor    <~~ push(makeAccessor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] AccKind <~~ push({akind: up.in}) ~~> [ AccParam ] [ FnEffects ] AccBody ;
AccKind     = ( "willSet" | "didSet" | "get" | "set" | "_modify" | "_read"
              | "unsafeAddress" | "unsafeMutableAddress" ) KwEnd ;
AccParam    = "(" Id <~~ push({aparam: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> ")" ;
AccBody     <~~ push({abody: pop()}) ~~> = FnBody ;
// `var family: String { "beast" }` - the braces ARE the getter body.
ImplicitGetter <~~ push(makeImplicitGetter(pop())) ~~> = FnBody ;

// An enum declares cases (bare, with a raw value, or with associated values) and,
// like a struct, methods / computed properties / static members. Its body is the
// SAME Member list a struct has plus CaseDecl, so an enum method is built by the
// very machinery a struct method is.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEnum Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeGenParams ] [ ":" EnumConform ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The FIRST entry of an enum's inheritance clause may be its raw-value type
// (`: Int`, `: String`); every entry there may equally be a PROTOCOL.
// The first entry is recorded BOTH ways: rawKind decides whether it is really a
// raw-value type, and the adoption ignores a name that is no protocol.
EnumConform = EnumRawType { "," EnumProto } ;
// A `~Copyable` entry suppresses a conformance, exactly as in InheritName.
EnumRawType <~~ push({eraw: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = [ "~" ] Type ;
EnumProto   <~~ push({inherit: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = [ "~" ] Type ;
// case a, b  /  case two = "II"  /  case circle(r: Int)  /  case rect(Int, Int)
CaseDecl    = KwCase ECase { "," ECase } [ ";" ] ;
ECase       <~~ push({ecase: makeECase(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({cname: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> [ ECaseAssoc | "=" ECaseRaw ] ;
ECaseAssoc  <~~ push({cassoc: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ECaseParam { "," ECaseParam } ] ")" ;
ECaseParam  <~~ push(makeECaseParam(takeAll())) ~~> = [ ECaseLabel ] Type <~~ push({cty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> ;
ECaseLabel  = Id <~~ push({clabel: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> ":" ;
ECaseRaw    <~~ push({craw: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;

// import / protocol / extension / typealias declarations.
UnsupDecl   = ImportDecl | ProtocolDecl | ExtensionDecl | TypeAliasDecl ;
// import Geom (or a submodule import 'import struct A.B'): the module name is resolved by
// resolveImport - a builtin module (core.stdlibImports) is a no-op, otherwise it maps to a
// project file (Geom -> Geom.swift, A.B -> A/B.swift) parsed and walked by core.importFile,
// whose declarations register in the shared scope like the main file's. The submodule kind
// (struct/class/...) is parsed and dropped; the dotted module path is what is resolved.
ImportDecl  <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwImport [ ImportKind ] ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportPath  <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id { "." ( Id | "*" ) } ;
ImportKind  = ( "typealias" | "struct" | "class" | "enum" | "protocol"
              | "func" | "let" | "var" | "inout" ) KwEnd ;
// A protocol is a named member table: its REQUIREMENT NAMES are recorded (that is
// all a requirement contributes - this front end does no conformance checking) and
// the default implementations an extension of the protocol adds are what a
// conforming type inherits. A type's inheritance clause adopts every protocol it
// names, and the adoption also records the conformance, which is what makes
// `x is P` answerable.
//
// THE NAMES MATTER: Swift dispatches
// a call made from inside a protocol extension DYNAMICALLY only for a name the
// protocol declares. A member the extension alone introduces is bound statically to
// the extension's own implementation even when the conforming type declares one.
ProtocolDecl <~~ push(makeProtocolStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwProtocol Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ]
              "{" { ProtoMember } "}" ;
// A requirement has no body, so it is matched BEFORE the member forms: `var name:
// String { get }` would otherwise read as a computed property whose getter returns
// the value of a variable called `get`.
ProtoMember = PoundIfProto | ProtoReq | Member ;
// A #if inside a PROTOCOL body. PoundIfMember (reached through Member) collects
// only Members, and a protocol REQUIREMENT is not one.
PoundIfProto <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyProto { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyProto }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyProto ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyProto <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = PoundSkip | { PoundIfProto | ProtoReq | Member } ;
ProtoReq    = AssocTypeDecl | [ MemberModifiers ] ( ProtoVar | ProtoFunc | ProtoInit | ProtoSubscript ) ;
AssocTypeDecl <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = "associatedtype" KwEnd Id [ ":" TypeList ] [ "=" Type ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoVar    <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Id <~~ push({preq: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> [ ":" Type ]
              "{" ProtoAcc { ProtoAcc } "}" ;
ProtoAcc    = ( "get" | "set" | "async" | "throws" ) KwEnd ;
ProtoFunc   <~~ swAnnParams([]); push({}) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName <~~ push({preq: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ParamList
              [ FnEffects ] [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoInit   <~~ swAnnParams([]); push({}) ~~>
            = KwInit [ "?" | "!" ] [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoSubscript <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = KwSubscript [ SkipAngle ] SkipParen "->" Type "{" ProtoAcc { ProtoAcc } "}" ;

// extension T { ... } adds members to an existing type - a struct/class/enum, a
// protocol (whose members become the defaults of every conforming type) or a
// builtin (Int, String, Array, ...), which keeps its members in a side table.
// `extension T: P` additionally adopts P.
ExtensionDecl <~~ push(makeExtensionStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwExtension ExtType [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ExtType     <~~ push({extname: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// typealias: an alias needs nothing at run time; its target text is recorded for
// the write-site adoption test (swTyUnalias).
AliasGenParams <~~ push({algens: swGenParamNames(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = SkipAngle ;
TypeAliasDecl <~~ push(swNoteAlias(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTypealias Id <~~ push({alname: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> [ AliasGenParams ] "="
              Type <~~ push({alty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> [ ";" ] ;

// Types are parsed and ignored: arrays [T], dictionaries [K: V], optionals T?,
// implicitly unwrapped T!, tuples/function types (A, B) -> C, dotted and generic,
// opaque/existential `some P` / `any P`, and compositions P & Q. The `&` of a
// composition is spelled ( "&" !"&" ) so `x is T && y` is not read as `T & (&y)`.
Type        = { TypeAttr } TypeMod TypeSuffixed { TypeAmp TypeMod TypeSuffixed } ;
// An attribute ON a type: `typealias F = @isolated(any) () -> ()`, `@Sendable () ->
// ()`, `@convention(c) () -> ()`. Its argument list is ADJACENT in Swift, which is
// what lets `@escaping (Int) -> Int` keep its function type - a SkipParen that
// accepted the space would swallow the parameter list.
TypeAttr    = "@" Id { "." Id } [ SkipAngle ] [ NoSpaceParen SkipParen ] ;
// The optional / implicitly-unwrapped marker has to be ADJACENT to the type:
// `x is T ? a : b` must keep its conditional, and `T?` never has a space in Swift.
// `.Type` / `.Protocol` after a parenthesized type names its metatype:
// `(P & Q).Type`.
// A leading `~` suppresses a conformance in a composition: `any ~Copyable`.
TypeSuffixed = [ "~" ] TypeCore { NoSpace ( "?" | "!" ) | "." ( "Type" | "Protocol" ) KwEnd } ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when whitespace (or end of file) is ahead - the
// dialect skips whitespace before each token, so "no space here" has to be asked
// explicitly.
NoSpace     = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        return (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc < 0)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01space-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
TypeAmp     = "&" !"&" ;
// The ownership and existential modifiers are CONTEXTUAL keywords, so each one is
// also a legal type NAME: `func zim(x: borrowing) {}` declares a parameter of a type
// called `borrowing`. The lookahead is what tells them apart - a modifier is always
// followed by the type it modifies, never by the token that ends one.
// `repeat each T` is a PACK EXPANSION type (SE-0393): `repeat` is a real keyword
// here and `each` a contextual one, and both only ever precede the type they
// modify - so they belong in the same modifier run as `some` / `any`.
// `sending` (SE-0430) marks a value handed across an isolation boundary; like the
// ownership modifiers it changes nothing a single-threaded run can observe.
TypeMod     = { ( "some" | "any" | "inout" | "borrowing" | "consuming" | "sending" | "each" | "repeat" ) KwEnd NotTypeEnd } ;
NotTypeEnd  = !")" !"," !"=" !">" !"{" ;
TypeCore    = "[" Type [ ":" Type ] "]"
            | "(" [ TupleTypeElem { "," TupleTypeElem } ] ")" [ TypeEffects ] [ "->" Type ]
            | Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
TupleTypeElem = [ TupleTypeLabel ] { ParamAttr } Type [ "..." ] ;
TupleTypeLabel = Id [ Id ] ":" ;

// The element names of a tuple live in its TYPE, not in the literal: for
//     let pair: (n: String, v: Int) = ("k", 9)
// pair.n has to work. AnnType is Type with that one extra job - when the
// annotation is a tuple type whose first element is labelled it pushes the label
// list, and the declaration (or the function return) copies those names onto the
// value. NotArrow declines a function type, whose parameter labels name nothing.
//
// AnnType also carries the second job DeclAnnType has: an `Int8`/`Double`/`Float`
// annotation pushes its {itype} so the RETURN TYPE of a function and the declared
// type of a STORED PROPERTY adopt it, exactly as a `let`/`var` annotation does.
// Every consumer of AnnType (makeFun, makeComputed, makeSubscript, ProtoFunc)
// skips or reads the record (a stored property's PropBind reads DeclAnnType); see swAdoptTy.
// FnAnnType is here for the same reason it is in DeclAnnType: a STORED PROPERTY
// and a RETURN TYPE put the function type on the slot, not on the value. It costs
// nothing at a PARAMETER, which reads its own ParamType rather than AnnType.
// A PARAMETER adopts through the same annotation text, structural forms included -
// `(xs: [Double])` and `(g: (Double) -> Double)` both adopt - but the gate is
// swAdoptNeeded, so an annotation with no adoptable leaf in it still emits nothing.
AnnType     = FnAnnType | LabelledTupleType | IntAnnType | OptNumAnnType | DeepAnnType | ScalarAnnType | Type ;
// The captured TEXT rides along beside the labels: a labelled tuple type declares
// its element types too, and `let t: (x: Double, y: Int) = (3, 4)` has to hold 3.0
// exactly as the anonymous `(Double, Int)` form does. swAdoptDeep strips each
// element's `label:` prefix.
LabelledTupleType <~~ push({tlabels: takeAll(), atype: swAliasExpand(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = "(" LtNamed { "," LtElem } ")" NotArrow NotTypeSuffix ;
LtElem      = LtNamed | LtAnon ;
LtNamed     = Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> ":" Type ;
LtAnon      <~~ push("") ~~> = Type ;
NotArrow    = !"->" ;
TypeEffects = { "throws" KwEnd [ ThrownType ] | ( "async" | "rethrows" ) KwEnd } ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
// AttrDecl is a declaration STATEMENT carrying an attribute - `@_noImplicitCopy let y = x`
// inside a body, `@A<Float> var test0: Int { ... }` at top level. It needs at
// least one `@`, so it can never be confused with a bare expression statement.
Statement   = AttrDecl StmtSep
            | PoundDirective StmtSep
            | Decl StmtSep | LocalFunc StmtSep | LocalType StmtSep | CplxIf StmtSep | If StmtSep
            | CplxGuard StmtSep | Guard StmtSep | CplxWhile StmtSep | While StmtSep
            | Repeat StmtSep | For StmtSep | Switch StmtSep | Return StmtSep | Throw StmtSep
            | Break StmtSep | Continue StmtSep | Fallthrough StmtSep | Do StmtSep
            | Defer StmtSep | Empty | TupleAssign StmtSep
            | ExprStmt StmtSep ;
// swiftc's "consecutive statements on a line must be separated by ';'": a statement
// ends at a `;` (its own, or the next token), a line break, a `}`, a comment or the
// end of the file. A zero-width guard; the parse position is right after the
// statement's last token, since whitespace is skipped BEFORE a token. Each
// alternative carries it, so one that only matched a PREFIX of the line gives way
// to the next instead of failing the statement.
StmtSep     = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(-1) === 59) { return undefined }
        var i = 0
        var ch = c.peek(i)
        while (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 11 || ch === 12) { i++; ch = c.peek(i) }
        if (ch < 0 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 59 || ch === 125) { return undefined }
        if (ch === 47 && (c.peek(i + 1) === 47 || c.peek(i + 1) === 42)) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01statement-continues-on-line", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

AttrDecl    = Attribute { Attribute } [ Modifiers ] ( Decl | LocalFunc | LocalType ) ;

// A loop (or switch) label: `outer: for ... { ... break outer }`. It is part of the
// loop production rather than a statement of its own, so the builder sees its own
// label and a labelled break/continue can name the loop it leaves.
LoopLabel   = Id <~~ push({lbl: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> ":" ;

// A nested function declaration inside a body: a closure over the enclosing scope
// bound to its own name (so it can recurse and can capture surrounding locals).
LocalFunc   <~~ push(makeLocalFunStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] FunDecl ;
// A nested type declaration inside a body - the same struct/class/enum productions
// as at the top level, run as a statement of the enclosing body.
LocalType   = StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl ;

// do { STMTS } catch [pattern] { STMTS } [catch ...]: genuine try/catch (Swift has
// NO finally). throw raises a value that unwinds to the nearest catch; the first
// catch clause whose pattern MATCHES wins; a catch with no
// `let NAME` binds the implicit name `error`. return/break/continue inside a do still
// propagate to the enclosing loop or function. Each part is marked so excTry can tell
// them apart on the stack.
Do          <~~ push(swTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDo TryBody { CatchClause } ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
// catch PATTERN[, PATTERN] [where COND] { ... }. The clause matches when one of its
// patterns matches the thrown value (a clause with no pattern matches anything), and
// that pattern's bindings - plus the implicit `error` - are live in the body. A bare
// `catch let e` is the pattern `let e`, which binds and always matches; so is
// `catch let VendErr.limit(k)`, which binds only for that case.
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatchClause(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch [ CatchPat { "," CatchPat } [ CatchWhere ] ] Block ;
// `catch let e as NetErr`: the `as TYPE` is carried on the pattern as `asty` and
// tested by matchPat/emitMatchPat with exactly the `is` operator's test, so a
// clause whose type does not match falls to the next clause, and
// `catch let e as T` and `catch is T` agree by construction.
CatchPat    <~~ push({cpat: pop()}) ~~> = Pattern ;
CatchWhere  <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;
// defer { ... }: the block is registered on the enclosing function's defer frame
// and runs when that function leaves - in LIFO order, on the normal path, on an
// early return AND on the way out of a throw (makeFunBody runs the frame from a
// host finally, so an unwinding exception still passes through it).
Defer       <~~ push(makeDefer(pop())) ~~>
            = KwDefer Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(excThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression [ ";" ] ;
// fallthrough: continue into the NEXT case's body without re-testing its pattern.
// It is a signal the enclosing switch consumes, like break/continue for a loop.
Fallthrough <~~ push(makeFallthrough()) ~~>
            = KwFallthrough [ ";" ] ;

// if / guard / while whose condition uses optional binding (if let x = ...),
// pattern matching (if case ...) or a compound comma condition. Plain boolean
// conditions fall through to If/Guard/While, which stay the cheaper single-thunk
// forms; a clause reaches here only when it carries a comma or a binding.
// A binding whose value is nil fails the whole clause; the names it binds live in
// a fresh scope for if/while and, Swift-style, in the ENCLOSING scope for guard
// (whose else branch is expected to leave that scope anyway).
CplxIf      <~~ push(makeCondIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf CplxCondClause Block [ KwElse ElseBody ] ;
CplxGuard   <~~ push(makeCondGuard(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwGuard CplxCondClause KwElse Block ;
CplxWhile   <~~ push(makeCondWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwWhile CplxCondClause Block ;
// A complex clause requires either a comma (2+ conditions) or a leading binding.
CplxCondClause <~~ push({conds: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = CondItem "," CondItem { "," CondItem } | BindCond { "," CondItem } ;
CondItem    = BindCond | CondExpr ;
CondExpr    <~~ push({cexpr: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
BindCond    = OptBind | CaseCond ;
// `if let x = e` and the 5.7 shorthand `if let x` (which rebinds x from itself).
OptBind     <~~ push(makeOptBind(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd BindPat [ ":" Type <~~ push({btype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> ]
              [ "=" CondInit <~~ push({ctext: up.in}) ~~> ] [ PatWhere ] ;
CaseCond    <~~ push(makeCaseCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Pattern "=" CondInit [ PatWhere ] ;
CondInit    <~~ push({cinit: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
PatWhere    <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;
BindPat     <~~ push({bnames: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" BindName { "," BindName } ")" | BindName ;
BindName    <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;

// A pattern: `_`, `let x`, `x?` (optional pattern), a tuple of patterns, or an
// enum case - `.busy`, `Load19.busy`, optionally with a sub-pattern argument list.
// A bare identifier is a BINDING when it sits under a `let`/`var` and an enum case
// reference otherwise, so matchCore carries that flag down the tree.
Pattern     <~~ push(makePattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatCore { NoSpace "?" <~~ push({popt: 1}) ~~> } [ "as" KwEnd PatAsType ] ;
// `case let d as Double` / `catch let e as NetErr`: a type-casting pattern, which
// matches when the subject `is` that type and then binds or tests it.
PatAsType   <~~ push({casty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
PatCore     = PatIs | PatLet | PatTuple | PatWild | PatRange | PatElem | PatKwLit | PatEnum | PatExpr ;
// `case nil:` / `case true:` compare with the literal; PatEnum would read the
// keyword as a case NAME and never match.
PatKwLit    <~~ push({pexpr: pop()}) ~~> = Nil | True | False ;
// `case is Double:` - a TYPE pattern. It runs exactly the test the `is`
// OPERATOR runs, so it agrees with `case let z where z is Double` by construction.
// It is tried FIRST: PatEnum would otherwise read the bare word `is` as a case
// name to compare the subject against, match, and leave the type standing.
PatIs       <~~ push({pis: pop()}) ~~> = "is" KwEnd PatIsType ;
PatIsType   <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Type ;
// Range patterns, including the one-sided forms: 1...9, 10..., ..<0. Tried before
// PatEnum and PatExpr, both of which would match the lower bound on its own and
// then leave the range operator behind.
PatRange    = PatRangeCC | PatRangeUp | PatRangeDn ;
PatRangeCC  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = PatBound RangeOp PatBound ;
PatRangeUp  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = PatBound RangeOp ;
PatRangeDn  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = RangeOp PatBound ;
// A range bound may also be a variable, a member or an element (`lo...xs[1]`):
// the range operator that must follow keeps a bare name from being read as one.
PatBound    = PatOperand | PatRef ;
PatRef      <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~> = VarRef { FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
// A value pattern compares the subject with ==. Its operand is deliberately NOT the
// full expression grammar: a `{ ... }` would be read as a closure and swallow the
// body of `catch { ... }`, whose pattern is optional.
// An element as a value pattern, `case xs[0]:`. The subscript is required, so a
// bare name or a qualified path still reaches PatEnum; tried before it, which would
// match `xs` alone and leave the subscript behind.
PatElem     <~~ push({pexpr: foldCallMember(takeAll(), up.in)}) ~~>
            = VarRef { FieldSfx } IndexSfx { FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
PatExpr     <~~ push({pexpr: pop()}) ~~> = PatOperand ;
PatOperand  = PatNegLit | PatQual | Template | NumberLit | True | False | Nil ;
PatNegLit   <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~> = "-" NumberLit ;
// A qualified static as a range bound: `6..<Int.max`, `Limits.lo...`.
PatQual     <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~> = VarRef FieldSfx { FieldSfx } ;
PatLet      <~~ push({plet: pop()}) ~~> = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Pattern ;
PatTuple    <~~ push({ptup: takeAll()}) ~~> = "(" Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" ;
PatWild     <~~ push({pwild: 1}) ~~> = Wildcard ;
PatEnum     <~~ push(makePatEnum(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ "." <~~ push({pdot: 1}) ~~> ] PatPath [ PatArgs ] ;
PatPath     <~~ push({ppath: swBt(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = Id { "." Id } ;
PatArgs     <~~ push({pargs: takeAll()}) ~~> = "(" [ PatArg { "," PatArg } ] ")" ;
PatArg      = [ PatArgLabel ] Pattern ;
PatArgLabel = Id ":" ;
Wildcard    = "_" KwEnd ;

Block       <~~ swAnnClose(); push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" <~~ swAnnOpen() ~~> { PoundIfStmt | Statement } "}" ;
RetBody     <~~ swAnnClose(); push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" <~~ swAnnOpen() ~~> Return { PoundIfStmt | Statement } "}" ;

Decl        = DeclTuple | LocalComputed | DeclPlain ;
// A computed variable at top level or in a body - `var t: Int { ... }`, a result
// builder's `@A var v: Int { 1; 2 }` - is recognized, and reported as not
// implemented: a READ of the name would have to run the getter.
LocalComputed <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("a local computed variable", up.start)) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd Id ":" Type SkipBlock ;
// One let/var may declare SEVERAL bindings: `let a = 1, b = 2`. Each binding builds
// its own declaration thunk and the statement runs them in order; `var a, b:
// Double` declares both as Double (swBindsShared).
DeclPlain   <~~ push(makeSeq(swBindsShared(takeAll(), makeDecl))) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd DeclBind { "," DeclBind } [ ";" ] ;
DeclBind    <~~ push({dbind: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> [ ":" DeclAnnType ] [ "=" Expression <~~ push({itext: up.in, iat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> ] [ LocalObs ] ;
// `var w = 0 { didSet { ... } }` on a top-level or local variable: the observers are
// the same accessors a property's are, and only willSet/didSet are accepted here.
LocalObs    <~~ push({lobs: takeAll()}) ~~> = "{" ObsAccessor { ObsAccessor } "}" ;
ObsAccessor <~~ push(makeAccessor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ObsKind <~~ push({akind: up.in}) ~~> [ AccParam ] AccBody ;
ObsKind     = ( "willSet" | "didSet" ) KwEnd ;
// The declaration's type annotation. It is DISCARDED everywhere except for the two
// things this front end can act on without a type checker: the element labels of a
// tuple type (LabelledTupleType), the WIDTH of an integer type - `var u: UInt8 =
// 250` has to make 250 a UInt8, because nothing about the value 250 says so and the
// `u &+ 10` that follows has to wrap at 8 bits - and the FLOATNESS of a Double type,
// for the same reason: `let d: Double = 3` holds 3.0, and nothing about the untyped
// literal 3 says so. IntAnnType is tried before the general Type; the names are
// spelled longest-first (PEG first-match: "Int" would otherwise match the front of
// "Int8") and closed with a KwEnd so a user type called `Int8Box` is not read as
// `Int8`.
DeclAnnType = FnAnnType | LabelledTupleType | IntAnnType | OptNumAnnType | DeepAnnType | ScalarAnnType | OtherAnnType ;
// Any other annotation is captured as TEXT only for the write-site check that an
// EXISTENTIAL slot (`Any`, a protocol, ...) does not adopt; see swKeepMode.
// Each annotation form keeps only its TEXT: a type attribute inside it
// (`(@escaping (Int) -> Int) -> Int`) pushes a marker that is dropped here. One
// applying a generic type of the program (`Box<Double>`) adopts, and one naming a
// typealias is read as the type it stands for: swOtherAnnRec.
OtherAnnType <~~ takeAll(); push(swOtherAnnRec(stripWs(up.in))) ~~> = Type ;
// A FUNCTION-TYPED SLOT. Every other adoption site in this front end reads the type
// off the VALUE (see chapter 7.15 of the manual) - floatness and integer width live
// on the value and a write adopts what the slot already holds. That argument runs
// out here: `let f: (Double) -> Double = { x in x/2 }` puts the type on the SLOT and
// the closure carries nothing, yet `f(3)` must divide a Double (1.5, not 1).
// The annotation is the only thing that says the argument is a Double, so
// it is captured (the general Type would discard it) and the declaration wraps the
// value in a synthetic function that adopts each argument, calls the original and
// adopts the result - swAdoptFn here, swEmitAdoptFn in the compiler.
FnAnnType   <~~ takeAll(); push({atype: swAliasExpand(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = "(" [ TupleTypeElem { "," TupleTypeElem } ] ")" [ TypeEffects ] "->" Type ;
IntAnnType  <~~ push({itype: up.in}) ~~> = NumTypeName NotTypeSuffix ;
NumTypeName = ( "Int64" | "Int32" | "Int16" | "Int8" | "Int"
              | "UInt64" | "UInt32" | "UInt16" | "UInt8" | "UInt"
              | "Double" | "Float" ) KwEnd ;
// The STRUCTURAL annotation forms, whose captured TEXT is walked by swAdoptDeep:
// an array `[Double]`, a dictionary `[String: Double]`, a tuple `(Double, Int)`
// and any of them nested, each carrying its numeric leaf down to the untyped
// literals inside the initializer (`let m: [String: Double] = ["a": 3]` holds 3.0).
//
// OptNumAnnType is the same rule for `var op: Double? = 1`: an Optional IS its
// value in this model, so the annotation adopts exactly as the bare name does.
// It is a rule of its own because IntAnnType must keep declining a composed type
// (NotTypeSuffix) - that guard is what stops `Int?` consuming only the `Int` and
// leaving the `?` to break the NEXT statement.
//
// The inner types are the general Type: only the top level has to be a container
// here, and swAdoptDeep ignores every leaf it does not recognise. A typealias in
// the text is expanded first (swAliasExpand in lib/swift-dotwant.js).
// `Unicode.Scalar`: a String literal it types becomes a scalar (swMkScalar).
ScalarAnnType <~~ takeAll(); push({atype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "Unicode" NoSpace "." NoSpace "Scalar" | "UnicodeScalar" ) KwEnd NotTypeSuffix ;
OptNumAnnType <~~ takeAll(); push({atype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = NumTypeName NoSpace ( "?" | "!" ) NotTypeSuffix ;
DeepAnnType <~~ takeAll(); push({atype: swAliasExpand(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = DeepTyCore { NoSpace ( "?" | "!" ) } NotTypeSuffix ;
DeepTyCore  = "[" Type [ ":" Type ] "]"
            | "(" TupleTypeElem { "," TupleTypeElem } ")" NotArrow ;
// `Int?`, `Int!`, `Int.Type`, `Int & P`: the annotation is a COMPOSED type, not the
// bare integer type, so IntAnnType has to decline it and let the general Type read
// it. Without this the optional forms consumed only the `Int` and left the `?`
// standing, which turned into a parse error on the NEXT statement.
NotTypeSuffix = !"?" !"!" !"." !"&" ;
// let (a, _, c) = someTuple - a tuple-pattern declaration; _ discards its element.
DeclTuple   <~~ push(makeDeclTuple(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd TupNames [ ":" DeclTupTy ] "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;
// `let (a, b): (Double, Int) = (1, 2)`: the annotation types the literal, so a is 1.0.
DeclTupTy   <~~ push({dtty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An element is a name, _ or a nested tuple pattern: `for (i, (x, y)) in`.
TupNames    <~~ push({tnames: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" TupName { "," TupName } ")" ;
TupName     = TupNames | TupLeaf ;
TupLeaf     <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;
// (x, y) = (y, x) - assignment through a tuple pattern (a swap, typically).
TupleAssign <~~ push(makeTupleAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TupNames "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf Expression Block [ KwElse ElseBody ] ;
ElseBody    = Block | CplxIf | If ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhileL(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwWhile Expression Block ;

Repeat      <~~ push(makeRepeat(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwRepeat Block KwWhile Expression [ ";" ] ;

// for x in 1...5 / 1..<5, and for x in array. The range form is tried first.
// `for case PAT in xs` filters: only the elements the pattern matches run the body
// (and the pattern's `let` names are bound for that iteration). Tried first - its
// `case` would otherwise be read as the loop variable's name by ForRange/ForList.
For         = ForCase | ForRange | ForList | ForAwait ;
// `for await x in seq` / `for try await` walk an AsyncSequence, which needs an
// executor this runtime does not have: recognized, reported as not implemented.
ForAwait    <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("for await", up.start)) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor [ KwTry ] KwAwait [ "var" KwEnd ] ForPat [ ForPatType ] KwIn
              Expression [ ForWhere ] Block ;
ForCase     <~~ push(makeForCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor KwCase Pattern KwIn Expression [ ForWhere ] Block ;
// The bounds are read at AddExpr level, BELOW RangeExpr, so the loop keeps its own
// range operator instead of receiving one range value.
// `for var x in xs` only makes x mutable, which every loop variable here already is.
// The inline tags hand the loop variable's annotation to the body (swForSeq).
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor [ "var" KwEnd ] ForPat <~~ swForPat(up.in) ~~> KwIn AddExpr RangeOp
              AddExpr <~~ swForSeq("") ~~> [ ForWhere ] Block ;
RangeOp     = "..." <~~ push({rop: "..."}) ~~>
            | "..<" <~~ push({rop: "..<"}) ~~> ;
ForList     <~~ push(makeForList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor [ "var" KwEnd ] ForPat <~~ swForPat(up.in) ~~> [ ForPatType ] KwIn
              Expression <~~ swForSeq(up.in) ~~> [ ForWhere ] Block ;
// The loop variable: a name, _ , or a tuple pattern (for (k, v) in aDictionary).
ForPat      = TupNames | ForName ;
ForName     <~~ push({fname: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;
// `for x: Double in xs` - the annotation types the loop variable (swForSeq).
// The element literal adopts it - `for x: Double in [1, 2]` walks 1.0 and 2.0 - so a
// simple name's annotation travels to makeForList as {fty}; a tuple pattern's
// annotation only restates the element type.
ForPatType  <~~ push({fty: swForAnn}) ~~> = ":" Type <~~ swForTy(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> ;
// for x in xs where COND { ... }: the body runs only for the elements that pass.
ForWhere    <~~ push({fwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;

// guard COND else { ... }: if COND is false the else block runs (and normally
// exits via return/break/continue, which propagates); otherwise control falls
// through past the guard.
Guard       <~~ push(makeGuard(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwGuard Expression KwElse Block ;

// switch SUBJ { case V1, V2: stmts  case V3: stmts  default: stmts } - lowered to
// a chain of == tests (no fallthrough). The body is parsed FLAT: the labels must
// be tried before Statement (a bare 'case' would parse as an identifier), and the
// builder groups the statements under the label they follow. A bare break ends the
// case; return/continue propagate to the function/enclosing loop.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwSwitch SwitchSubj "{" { PoundIfCase | CaseLabel | Statement } "}" ;
SwitchSubj  <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// A case label is a comma-separated list of PATTERNS with an optional where guard:
// values (case 1, 2), ranges (case 1...9, case ..<0), tuples (case (0, let y)),
// enum cases with their associated values (case .rect(let w, let h)) and bindings
// (case let (x, y) where x == y). Compound labels may bind the same name in each
// alternative, so the bindings of whichever alternative matched are the live ones.
CaseLabel   <~~ push(makeCaseLabel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( KwCase CaseItem { "," CaseItem } [ CaseWhere ]
              | KwDefault ) ":" ;
CaseItem    <~~ push({cpat: pop()}) ~~> = Pattern ;
CaseWhere   <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression <~~ push({itext: up.in, iat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakL(takeAll())) ~~>    = KwBreak [ SameLine BreakLbl ] [ ";" ] ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinueL(takeAll())) ~~> = KwContinue [ SameLine BreakLbl ] [ ";" ] ;
BreakLbl    <~~ push({lbl: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> = Id ;

// A zero-width guard that fails at end of line (a newline or a // comment before the
// next token): the label of `break outer` must start on the SAME line, otherwise a
// bare `break` followed by a statement would swallow that statement's first name.
SameLine    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc < 0 || (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47))
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01newline-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// `;` alone, or `()` - the empty tuple as a statement (`case 0: ()`): a no-op.
Empty       <~~ push(function() {}) ~~> = ";" | "(" ")" StmtSep ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression [ ";" ] ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
Expression  = PackRepeat | Assignment | Ternary ;
// `repeat <expr>` - a pack expansion EXPRESSION (SE-0393). It evaluates its body
// once per element of the packs `each` names inside it and yields the results as a
// tuple, which is what makes `(repeat 2 * each arg + 3)` a tuple of the same arity
// as the value pack. `repeat` is a reserved word, and the repeat/while STATEMENT is
// matched before ExprStmt, so no guard is needed to tell the two apart.
PackRepeat  <~~ push(makePackRepeat(pop())) ~~> = KwRepeat Expression ;
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              Expression <~~ push({itext: up.in, iat: up.start + ":" + up.pos}) ~~> ;

// An assignment TARGET is a name followed by field reads, tuple-element reads,
// force-unwraps and subscripts, in any order: `n.next!.v = 4` and `tp.0 = 3`.
//
// TForce is spelled with the same NoSpace/NotChar61 guard ForceSfx uses, and the
// guard is what makes the rule safe: Assignment is tried before Ternary, so
// without the "no = next" test `a != b` would read as the target `a!` followed by
// `= b` and silently become an assignment. A `!` here is otherwise a no-op - an
// Optional IS its value in this model - so it pushes nothing.
Target      <~~ push(swNoteWrite(swOptEndRef(makeTargetRef(takeAll())))) ~~>
            = ( Id <~~ push(swBt(up.in)) ~~> | "$" :whitespace() TShort :whitespace(Whitespace) )
              { TOpt | "." Id <~~ push({key: swBt(up.in)}) ~~> | "." TTupIdx | TForce | TIndex } [ TOptEnd ] ;
// `counts[w]? += 1` / `x? = v`: a trailing `?` makes the whole assignment skip,
// right-hand side included, when the target itself is nil (swOptEndRef).
TOptEnd     <~~ push({optend: true}) ~~> = NoSpace "?" NotChar63 ;
// `a?.b = c` / `a?[i] = c`: an optional-chain target. The segment after the `?`
// carries `opt`: a nil there skips the whole assignment, right-hand side included.
TOpt        = NoSpace "?" NotChar63 ( "." Id <~~ push({key: swBt(up.in), opt: true}) ~~> | TOptIdx ) ;
TOptIdx     <~~ var ia = takeAll(); push({idx: makeIndexKeyL(swIdxKeys(ia), swIdxLabelOf(ia)), dflt: swIdxDfltOf(ia), opt: true}) ~~>
            = "[" IndexArg { "," IndexArg } "]" ;
TTupIdx     <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
TShort      <~~ push("$" + up.in) ~~> = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
